<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Augustus, Duke of Nassau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Augustus was prince of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> from 1803 and, when it united with Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> in 1806, the first duke of Nassau within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>. He shared government with his cousin Frederick William of Nassau-Weilburg, and the duchy kept its gains when it changed sides in 1813. When he died without surviving sons in 1816 the Usingen line ended, and Nassau passed to the Weilburg line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-duke-of-nassau.jpg" alt="Frederick Augustus, Duke of Nassau" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_August_(Nassau-Usingen).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
